Social & Environmental
Incorrect medication administration, Teach-back failure, Low REALM score

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-020
Reason for Visit: Acute cardiovascular compromise secondary to profound, unintentional medication mismanagement.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an elderly patient experiencing extreme dizziness, near-syncope, and severe bradycardia after taking "all the new pills the specialist gave." Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for acute iatrogenic drug toxicity and prevent cardiovascular collapse prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Every time I stand up, the room spins and I black out. I took all my heart pills like the doctor said."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 12-hour history of profound presyncope, lethargy, and a sensation of a very slow heart rate. Key symptoms stem from severe health literacy issues. The patient demonstrates a consistent inability to accurately name prescribed medications or their purposes. There is clear evidence of incorrect administration of medications. The patient nods and expresses understanding during questioning but completely fails the teach-back method regarding dosing instructions. The spouse admits to incompletely filling out intake paperwork and notes the patient frequently says "I forgot my glasses" when asked to read medication labels.
Functional Status: Independent but acutely immobilized by severe symptomatic bradycardia/hypotension
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of fatal accidental overdose, complete lack of understanding of complex cardiovascular regimen

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/50 | HR: 42 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Pale, lethargic, lying flat on the couch, unable to safely stand
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals profound sinus bradycardia with no murmurs. Postural blood pressure check was abandoned due to immediate presyncope upon sitting. Neurological exam is grossly intact but patient is sluggish. Diagnostic tetrad features are starkly present: incorrect medication administration (taking beta-blockers and calcium channel blockers simultaneously at triple doses), complete teach-back failure, and obvious functional illiteracy regarding medical instructions.
Environmental Observations: Kitchen table covered in loose pills, recently opened boxes of Metoprolol and Diltiazem with multiple blister strips empty.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient unintentionally took three days' worth of newly prescribed AV-nodal blocking agents in a single morning dose, believing they were to be taken "three times all at once."

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Health literacy issues (Acute iatrogenic bradycardia/hypotension) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Acute Myocardial Infarction (Inferior) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of chest pain, diaphoresis, or radiation, and the clear, witnessed mechanism of massive, accidental AV-nodal blocker ingestion.
2. Vasovagal Syncope - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The persistence of profound bradycardia and hypotension while lying completely flat, rather than a transient, self-resolving event.
3. Sick Sinus Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The direct chronological correlation with the massive overdose of negative chronotropic medications in a patient with previously normal baseline heart rates.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in acute, life-threatening cardiogenic shock (symptomatic bradycardia and severe hypotension) directly resulting from the unintentional overdose of newly prescribed cardiovascular medications due to profound health illiteracy. Deferring this assessment would have led to complete heart block or fatal asystole. The objective findings of a heart rate of 42 bpm and systolic BP of 85 mmHg absolutely justify the urgent after-hours MBS intervention to immediately assess the severity of the toxicity and arrange emergency transport for cardiac monitoring and potential chronotropic support.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate positioning of the patient flat with legs elevated to maximize venous return. Withholding all further oral intake. Urgent dispatch of an intensive care ambulance.
Positive Behavioral Change: By using a weekly, pre-packed Webster-pak from the pharmacy instead of loose pill boxes, you completely remove the need to read complex labels, ensuring you take the exact right pill at the exact right time without any confusion.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mandatory referral to community pharmacy for dose administration aid (Webster-pak) implementation upon hospital discharge.
DME/Safety (Optional): None
Follow-up: Handover to Emergency Department team for cardiac monitoring and potential administration of Atropine or Glucagon.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department via ambulance. If the patient loses consciousness or stops breathing before the ambulance arrives, the spouse must immediately commence CPR.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The extreme slow heart rate is due to taking too much medication at once. The hospital will monitor your heart and may give you an antidote until the pills wear off.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to sit up, stand, or walk to the bathroom. You will pass out and injure yourself. Wait flat on your back until the paramedics arrive.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because the instructions were confusing, you accidentally took a toxic dose of your heart-slowing medications. This has caused your blood pressure to drop dangerously low.
Actionable Steps: Do not take any more pills from those boxes. When you leave the hospital, you must ask the pharmacist to put all your medications into a blister pack so you never have to read the tiny boxes again.

